            <ArticleTitle>Dental Trauma Part 1- Acute Management of Luxation/Displacement Injuries.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>812-4, 817-8, 820-22, 823</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Fortunately, traumatic dental injuries are a relatively uncommon occurrence in general dental practice. However, when they do present, timely diagnosis and treatment of such injuries is essential to maximize the chance of a successful outcome. This is the first part of a two-part series on traumatic dental injuries that are commonly encountered in the clinical setting. Part one will cover acute management of luxation/displacement injuries that primarily affect the supporting structures of the tooth, while part two will cover the management of fracture injuries associated with teeth and the alveolar bone. Clinical relevance: A simple, step-by-step approach in the diagnosis and clinical management of acute luxation/displacement injuries should be part of a dental clinician's knowledge.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
